⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the dispatcher report contradicts itself for suppressed bounces**

`gateRouteForAlternation` pushes the suppressed verdict into `routes.maintained`:

```js
// js/tcDifferentialDispatcher.js:398-402
if (suppression) {
    var suppressed = tcAlternation.suppressedVerdict(verdict, suppression);
    ...
    routes.maintained.push(suppressed);          // ← lands in the `maintained` bucket
    routes.suppressedBounces.push({ ... });
```

and `buildReportComment` renders a fixed template for every `maintained` item:

```js
// js/tcDifferentialDispatcher.js:472-473
if (routes.maintained.length) sections.push('The scripted differential rerun (gh-1345) ran the failing TC on its last-green build and it fails there too — ' +
    'the failure predates the change; the finding stays in the TC maintenance ledger.');
```

A suppressed bounce is the GREEN-on-old case (the rerun **confirmed** the regression), so for every suppressed request the ticket gets BOTH sections: *"…the bounce is suppressed (gh-1331) — a loop-stop anchor owns it"* **and** *"…it fails there too — the failure predates the change"*. The second sentence is factually wrong about the key evidence and will mislead the owner reading the report.

Fix: keep suppressed verdicts out of `routes.maintained` (or make the maintained section render per-item text chosen by `v.suppression`), so a suppressed route produces only the suppressed section. The `test_tcDifferentialDispatcher.js` gh-1331 fixture asserts `maintainedTestCases` but never asserts the rendered comment — add an assertion that the "fails there too" line is absent for suppressed routes.

⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the open-ledger anchor disarms itself after its first use — `suppressedVerdict` rewrites the finding's verdict to `maintenance`, which the `owns` check then rejects**

`openLedgerAnchor` only accepts findings that "own" the failure:

```js
// js/common/tcAlternation.js:302-303
var owns = finding.verdict === 'bounce' || finding.verdict === 'escalate' ||
    finding.source === tcMaintenanceLedger.SOURCE_DEV;
```

But when the suppression fires, the verdict appended/refreshed into the ledger is the *converted* one — `suppressedVerdict` sets `ledgerEntry.verdict = ACTION_MAINTENANCE` (`'maintenance'`) with reason `suppressed-*`:

```js
// js/common/tcAlternation.js:355-360
v.action = ACTION_MAINTENANCE;
v.reason = reason;
if (v.ledgerEntry) {
    v.ledgerEntry.verdict = ACTION_MAINTENANCE;
    v.ledgerEntry.reason = reason;
}
```

`appendFinding` → `refreshFinding` then overwrites the prior `bounce` finding's verdict with `'maintenance'`. On a later lap over the same range, that finding no longer matches `owns` → the `open-ledger-item` anchor silently stops matching.

Today this is masked for fresh laps because the registry bounce anchor (`lastBounceRange`, anchor 2) covers the same range — but the ledger anchor is the only line of defense exactly when the registry anchor is absent, i.e. registries persisted by **pre-gh-1331** markers (no `lastBounceRange`) or a marker whose posting failed (`postMarkerQuietly` is best-effort). In that state the third lap re-bounces the bug — the very ping-pong this PR exists to stop.

Fix: make the `owns` predicate recognize the suppression itself, e.g. also accept `String(finding.reason || '').indexOf('suppressed-') === 0` (or add an explicit `finding.verdict === 'maintenance' && finding.reason` prefix table). One unit test: ledger finding already refreshed to `verdict='maintenance', reason='suppressed-prior-bounce-same-range'` on the same range, registry WITHOUT `lastBounceRange` → `bounceSuppression` still returns `open-ledger-item`.

💡 **SUGGESTION: the two surfaces treat ACTION_ESCALATE under suppression inconsistently**

In the dispatcher gate, the suppression branch runs for **both** bounce and escalation verdicts — a cap escalation (`bounce-cap-reached`, the guard failed `maxReworkRounds` times) is converted to maintenance and `routes.escalated` never receives it:

```js
// js/tcDifferentialDispatcher.js:398-407
if (suppression) {
    var suppressed = tcAlternation.suppressedVerdict(verdict, suppression);   // action escalate → maintenance
    ...
    return;                                                                    // escalation swallowed
}
if (verdict.action === tcTriage.ACTION_BOUNCE) routes.bounced.push(verdict);
else routes.escalated.push(verdict);
```

The automation leg (`gateBounceForAlternation`) is the opposite: it gates only `ACTION_BOUNCE` and lets `ACTION_ESCALATE` pass through ungated (it just stamps `kind: 'escalated'`).

Same input (open pair conflict + cap escalation), different owner surface depending on which leg processed the lap: the dispatcher leg loses the "bounce cap reached" fact entirely (the pair escalation was posted once and never mentions the cap). If intentional (the pair escalation subsumes the cap), say so in a comment; otherwise gate only `ACTION_BOUNCE` here too, mirroring `gateBounceForAlternation`.

